    async def create_instance(
        self, project_id: str, body: models.PublicCloudInstanceInstanceCreation
    ) -> models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance:
        """create_instance calls POST /v2/publicCloud/project/{projectId}/compute/instance: Create an instance

        Provisions a new instance from the supplied `targetSpec`. Creation is asynchronous: the
        resource starts in `CREATING` and becomes `READY` once the backend has booted it. Poll `GET
        .../instance/{instanceId}` until `resourceStatus` is `READY` and `currentTasks` is empty.

        Create-time-only fields (immutable afterward)

        Several fields can be set only at creation and can never be changed later:

        - `location` — the region (and optional availability zone) the instance is deployed in.
        - `sshKeyName` — the SSH key injected into the instance at boot.
        - `group` — placement / instance-group membership. It binds the instance to an existing
          instance group (Nova server group) for `AFFINITY` / `ANTI_AFFINITY` scheduling.
          Server-group membership is fixed by OpenStack at boot and cannot be added, removed, or
          changed afterward — there is no group `PUT`.

        User data (cloud-init)

        `targetSpec.userData` is a base64-encoded (strict standard encoding) cloud-init payload
        handed to the instance at boot through the OpenStack metadata service / config drive, and
        interpreted by cloud-init inside the guest image. The encoded string must be at most 65535
        bytes. It is write-only: the value is never returned by `GET`/`LIST`. It is also not
        persisted or stored by the Public Cloud API: the payload is handed to the compute service,
        which makes it available to the instance via the metadata service. Only a non-reversible
        fingerprint of the value is retained, for change detection. Unlike the create-time-only
        fields above, `userData` can be changed later via `PUT`. See the `PUT` route for the
        destructive semantics of doing so.

        Declarative `volumes`

        `targetSpec.volumes` is a declarative list of block-volume references attached to the
        instance. Supply the volumes to attach at boot. On a subsequent `PUT` the list is diffed to
        attach or detach volumes (see the `PUT` route). There is no imperative attach/detach
        endpoint.

        Declarative `shares`

        `targetSpec.shares` is a declarative list of filesystem (Manila share) attachments. Each
        entry references a share plus an optional per-entry `accessLevel` (defaults to
        `READ_WRITE`). At creation, each attach creates a Manila IP access rule for the instance's
        interface on the share's network. The instance must have an interface on that network. As
        with volumes, the list is reconciled by diffing on `PUT`.

        Declarative `networks` and public IPs

        `targetSpec.networks` is a declarative list of network interfaces. Each entry is `{id?,
        subnetId?, ip?, autoAssignPublicIp?}`, and the fields you set decide what it means:

        - `{"autoAssignPublicIp": true}` — a public (Ext-Net) interface with a platform-assigned
          public IP. At most one entry may set it.
        - `{"ip": "..."}` without a network `id` — a public IP the project already owns: either an
          existing additional IP (also known as a failover IP), or an Ext-Net IP of the project in
          the instance's region. Any other address is rejected. Such an entry implicitly attaches
          the public (Ext-Net) interface. Several are allowed, and they may coexist with an
          `autoAssignPublicIp` entry.
        - `{"id": "...", "subnetId": "..."}` — a private interface whose fixed address is assigned
          by IPAM.
        - `{"id": "...", "subnetId": "...", "ip": "..."}` — a private interface where `ip` pins the
          fixed address when it falls inside the subnet CIDR, and otherwise designates an existing
          floating IP to associate with that interface (the floating IP must already exist).

        Any entry that sets `autoAssignPublicIp` or carries a public `ip` requires the optional
        `instance/usePublicIP` IAM action, including floating IP associations.

        In `currentState.networks` the shape is different: there is one entry per network plus at
        most one id-less entry for the public (Ext-Net) interface, and every address is reported
        through that entry's `addresses` list. There is no scalar `ip`. Each address carries a
        `type`: `FIXED` for an address configured on the interface itself (a private fixed address,
        whether IPAM-assigned or pinned, and the Ext-Net address), `FLOATING` for a floating IP
        associated with the interface, and `ADDITIONAL` for an additional IP routed to the public
        interface (additional IPs no longer form their own entry). `autoAssignPublicIp` is never
        echoed back, because the backend cannot distinguish a platform-assigned address from a
        pinned one.

        Args:
            project_id: Project ID
            body: the request body

        Requires the IAM action publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/create. May also use
        publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/usePublicIP.
        """
        return await self._call(
            _r.OP_CREATE_INSTANCE,
            path_params={
                "projectId": project_id,
            },
            body=body,
            body_type=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstanceCreation,
            result=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance,
        )

    async def get_instance(
        self, project_id: str, instance_id: str
    ) -> models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance:
        """get_instance calls GET /v2/publicCloud/project/{projectId}/compute/instance/{instanceId}: Get an instance

        Returns a single Public Cloud instance, including its desired `targetSpec` and observed
        `currentState`.

        Optimistic concurrency

        The `checksum` returned here is the hash of the current `targetSpec`. Keep it and send it
        back on any subsequent `PUT` or imperative `action` call on the instance to guard against
        lost updates. It is optional, but omitting it forces the write (see the `PUT` route).

        Filesystem attachments

        `currentState.shares` — the list of filesystems (Manila shares) attached to the instance —
        is populated only on this single-instance `GET`. It is deliberately not returned by the
        `LIST` route for performance reasons. Fetch the individual instance when you need it.

        Args:
            project_id: Project ID
            instance_id: Instance ID

        Requires the IAM action publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/get.
        """
        return await self._call(
            _r.OP_GET_INSTANCE,
            path_params={
                "projectId": project_id,
                "instanceId": instance_id,
            },
            result=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance,
        )

    async def edit_instance(
        self, project_id: str, instance_id: str, body: models.PublicCloudInstanceInstanceUpdate
    ) -> models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance:
        """edit_instance calls PUT /v2/publicCloud/project/{projectId}/compute/instance/{instanceId}: Update an instance

        This is the single, declarative route for changing an instance. You do not call
        verb-specific endpoints. Instead you submit a new desired `targetSpec` and the API derives
        the operations to perform from the diff against the current specification. (Imperative
        power/lifecycle operations such as reboot, start, stop, and rescue live on the separate
        `.../instance/{instanceId}/action` route.)

        Asynchronous, desired-state model

        1. You send the new `targetSpec`, optionally together with the `checksum` from your last
           `GET`.
        2. If accepted, you immediately get back the instance with the updated `targetSpec` and a
           new `checksum`. `resourceStatus` becomes `UPDATING`.
        3. One or more asynchronous tasks reconcile `currentState` toward `targetSpec`. Poll `GET
           .../instance/{instanceId}` until `resourceStatus` is `READY` and `currentTasks` is empty
           to know the change has been applied.

        Operations derived from the diff

        - Rename — changing `name` renames the instance.
        - Resize (flavor) — changing `flavor` migrates the instance to the new flavor.
        - Volume attach / detach — done declaratively by diffing the `volumes` list: adding a volume
          reference attaches that volume, removing one detaches it. There is no imperative
          attach/detach endpoint — the diff is the only mechanism.
        - Network attach / detach — done declaratively by diffing the `networks` list, whose entries
          are `{id?, subnetId?, ip?, autoAssignPublicIp?}`: a platform-assigned Ext-Net IP
          (`{"autoAssignPublicIp": true}`, at most one entry), a public IP the project already owns
          — additional IP or project Ext-Net IP in the instance's region — (`{"ip"}`, no network
          `id`), a private interface with an IPAM address (`{"id", "subnetId"}`), or a private
          interface with `ip` either pinning its fixed address (address inside the subnet CIDR) or
          associating an existing floating IP (address outside it). Any entry that sets
          `autoAssignPublicIp` or carries a public `ip` requires the optional `instance/usePublicIP`
          IAM action.
        - Filesystem (share) attach / detach — done declaratively by diffing the `shares` list.
          Adding an entry attaches the share: it creates a Manila IP access rule for the instance's
          interface on the share's network, so the instance must already have an interface on that
          network or the attach errors. Removing an entry detaches the share: the matching access
          rule is removed, and the detach is idempotent. Each entry's `accessLevel` defaults to
          `READ_WRITE`. Caveat: managing per-share access rules directly on the share resource *and*
          attaching the same share instance-side here is unsupported — the share will report
          `OUT_OF_SYNC`.

        Immutable fields (excluded from update)

        `location`, `sshKeyName`, and `group` are set only at creation and are not part of the
        update. In particular, placement-group (`group`) membership cannot change post-create.
        OpenStack/Nova server groups are immutable and there is deliberately no group `PUT`.

        Destructive update (`userData`)

        Changing `targetSpec.userData` reinstalls the instance — a Nova rebuild on its current image
        — which wipes the root disk and all data on it. Semantics are tri-state: omitting the field
        (or sending `null`) keeps the current value unchanged, an empty string `""` clears it, and
        any other value replaces it. Both the clear and the replace trigger the reinstall. An
        instance with no `image` (boot-from-volume) has nothing to rebuild onto, so a `userData`
        change on it is rejected with a `400 InvalidParameter`.

        Optimistic concurrency (`checksum`)

        The `checksum` guards against lost updates and is optional. When you send it, it must match
        the server-side value: if another request modified the instance since your last `GET`, the
        checksum no longer matches and the request is rejected with a checksum-mismatch error
        (`ChecksumMismatch`). Re-`GET` the instance and retry against the fresh state. When you omit
        it (or send an empty string), the check is skipped and the update is forced
        (last-write-wins).

        Args:
            project_id: Project ID
            instance_id: Instance ID
            body: the request body

        Requires the IAM action publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/edit. May also use
        publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/usePublicIP.
        """
        return await self._call(
            _r.OP_EDIT_INSTANCE,
            path_params={
                "projectId": project_id,
                "instanceId": instance_id,
            },
            body=body,
            body_type=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstanceUpdate,
            result=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance,
        )

    async def delete_instance(
        self, project_id: str, instance_id: str
    ) -> models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance:
        """delete_instance calls DELETE /v2/publicCloud/project/{projectId}/compute/instance/{instanceId}: Delete an instance

        Permanently deletes a Public Cloud instance. Deletion is asynchronous: the resource
        transitions to `DELETING`, the response echoes the instance, and the backend then removes
        it. Poll `GET .../instance/{instanceId}` until it returns `InstanceDoesNotExist` (`404`) to
        confirm removal.

        Behavior

        - Independent block volumes attached to the instance are detached, not deleted — they are
          separate resources and survive.
        - Filesystem (share) attachments are released as part of the teardown.

        A delete already in progress is reported as `ResourceAlreadyBeingDeleted`. An instance in a
        transient or otherwise invalid state may be rejected with `ResourceInInvalidState`.

        Args:
            project_id: Project ID
            instance_id: Instance ID

        Requires the IAM action publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/delete.
        """
        return await self._call(
            _r.OP_DELETE_INSTANCE,
            path_params={
                "projectId": project_id,
                "instanceId": instance_id,
            },
            result=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance,
        )

    async def post_instance_action(
        self,
        project_id: str,
        instance_id: str,
        body: models.PublicCloudInstanceInstanceActionRequest,
    ) -> models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance:
        """post_instance_action calls POST /v2/publicCloud/project/{projectId}/compute/instance/{instanceId}/action: Run an instance action

        Perform an instance action

        Imperative endpoint for power and lifecycle actions on an instance. This is the one place
        such imperative operations live. Distinct from the declarative `PUT` on the instance, which
        reconciles desired state from a `targetSpec` diff. Actions are asynchronous: the instance
        transitions through the relevant transient state and the response echoes the instance. Poll
        `GET .../instance/{instanceId}` until `resourceStatus` is `READY` and `currentTasks` is
        empty.

        Action `type`

        The `type` field selects the operation:

        - `REBOOT` — reboot the instance (`parameters.hard` chooses a hard vs. soft reboot).
        - `START` / `STOP` — power the instance on / off.
        - `SHELVE` / `UNSHELVE` — shelve the instance (freeing hypervisor resources) and restore it.
        - `PAUSE` / `UNPAUSE` — freeze a running instance in the memory of the host, and unfreeze
          it.
        - `SUSPEND` / `RESUME` — save the memory state of a running instance to disk and stop it,
          and restore it.
        - `RESCUE` / `UNRESCUE` — boot into rescue mode (optionally from `parameters.imageId`) and
          return to normal boot.
        - `LOCK` / `UNLOCK` — lock the instance against accidental actions / modifications, and
          unlock it.

        Optimistic concurrency (`checksum`)

        The `checksum` is optional. When you send it, it must match the current server-side value or
        the action is rejected with a checksum-mismatch error. Re-`GET` and retry. When you omit it
        (or send an empty string), the check is skipped and the action is forced (last-write-wins).
        An action requested while the instance is in an incompatible state is rejected with
        `ResourceInInvalidState`.

        Args:
            project_id: Project ID
            instance_id: Instance ID
            body: the request body

        Requires the IAM action publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/runAction.
        """
        return await self._call(
            _r.OP_POST_INSTANCE_ACTION,
            path_params={
                "projectId": project_id,
                "instanceId": instance_id,
            },
            body=body,
            body_type=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstanceActionRequest,
            result=models.PublicCloudInstanceInstance,
        )

    def all_instance_compatible_flavors(
        self, project_id: str, instance_id: str
    ) -> t.AsyncIterator[models.PublicCloudReferenceInstanceFlavor]:
        """all_instance_compatible_flavors iterates over GET /v2/publicCloud/project/{projectId}/compute/instance/{instanceId}/compatibleFlavor (List compatible flavors).

        It yields one item at a time, and gets the next page only when the loop reaches it.
        An error is raised and stops the loop. Use it with ``async for``.

        Compatible flavors

        Returns the list of flavors this instance can be resized to. The list is computed from the
        instance's current flavor and the catalog of flavors available in the instance's region.

        The following rules apply to the returned list:

        - It excludes the instance's current flavor.
        - It includes only flavors whose disk size is greater than or equal to the current flavor's
          disk size (a resize cannot shrink the root disk).
        - It includes only flavors in the same region as the instance.
        - It includes only flavors whose plan is available in the instance's region.
        - It is empty for baremetal instances, which cannot be resized.

        Each item has the same shape as an entry from the flavor reference catalog
        (`publicCloud.reference.instance.Flavor`). Its `location.regionActivated` is true, since the
        instance's region is activated on the project. `location.availabilityZones` lists the
        availability zones of the region, for example eu-west-par-a, eu-west-par-b and
        eu-west-par-c, and is empty for a single-AZ region.

        Args:
            project_id: Project ID
            instance_id: Instance ID

        Requires the IAM action publicCloudProject:apiovh:instance/compatibleFlavor/get.
        """
        return self._pages(
            _r.OP_ALL_INSTANCE_COMPATIBLE_FLAVORS,
            path_params={
                "projectId": project_id,
                "instanceId": instance_id,
            },
            item=models.PublicCloudReferenceInstanceFlavor,
        )

